#heading(level: 2)[
  Basic Elements, Stable Range, and Cancellation
] <sec:basic-elements-stable-range>

Before plunging into some rather complicated proofs, let us state some of the
main results of this section for the special case in which $Lambda$ is an
$R$-order in a f.d. $K$-algebra $A$, where $R$ is a Dedekind domain with
quotient field $K != R$. Let $P$ range over the maximal ideals of $R$, and let
$R_P$ denote localization at $P$. For $M, N$ $Lambda$-modules, write $M | N$ to
indicate that $M$ is a direct summand of $N$ (up to isomorphism).
#nidx(
  "$M divides N$",
  "means $M$ is isomorphic to a direct summand of $N$",
  target: [@sec:basic-elements-stable-range],
  chapter: [@ch:block-theory],
)

#enum(
  numbering: "(1)",
  [*Serre’s Theorem.*#idx("Serre's Theorem")#footnote[
      When $A$ is a separable $K$-algebra over a global field $K$, much stronger
      versions of these theorems are available. The Roiter–Jacobinski Theorem
      @i:th:roiter-local-direct-summand improves Serre’s Theorem, while the
      Jacobinski Cancellation Theorem is stronger than that of Bass.
    ]<fn:serre-cancellation-global> _Let_ $M in cal(P)(Lambda)$ _be such that_
    $ Lambda_P | M_P quad "for each" P, quad "and" A^((2)) | K M $
    _Then_ $Lambda | M$.],
  [*Bass Cancellation Theorem.*#idx("Bass Cancellation Theorem")#footnote(
      <fn:serre-cancellation-global>,
    ) _Let_ $M in cal(P)(Lambda)$ _be as above, and let_
    $L, N in cal(P)(Lambda)$.
    _Then_
    $ L plus.o N tilde.equiv M plus.o N => L tilde.equiv M. $],
  [*Swan–Forster Theorem.*#idx("Swan-Forster Theorem") _Let_ $mu_Lambda lr((M))$
    _denote the minimal number of generators of an arbitrary f.g._
    $Lambda$-_module_ $M$. _Then_
    $
      mu_Lambda lr((M)) <= op("Max")_P {mu_(Lambda_P) lr((M_P)),
        1 + mu_A lr((K times.o_R M))}.
    $],
  [*Bass Stable Range Theorem.*#idx("Bass Stable Range Theorem") $Lambda$ has
    stable range 2.],
)

As we shall see below, these results are special cases of more general theorems,
dealing with the case where $R$ is a commutative ring of finite Krull dimension,
and $Lambda$ is an $R$-algebra, f.g./$R$ as module. Some of the proofs will be
carried out in the more general situation, but on the whole we shall avoid the
machinery of commutative ring theory, and restrict attention to the Dedekind
domain case when it seems appropriate. Our approach is by means of basic
elements, as developed in Eisenbud–Evans @bib:Eisenbud1973. The authors wish to
thank Michael Fry for helpful comments on the material in this section.

Throughout this section we assume that all modules are f.g. If $M$ is a
$B$-module for some ring $B$, let
$
  cases(
    mu_B lr((M)) = "minimal number of generators of" M,
    lambda_B lr((M)) = "length of a composition series of" M,
  )
$ <eq:module-generator-number-length>
assuming $M$ has a composition series. We often omit the subscript $B$ when
there is no danger of confusion.
#source(100)

#definition[
  Let $M' subset.eq M$ be $A$-modules, where $A$ is a semisimple ring. Call $M'$
  _$t$-fold basic_ in $M$ if
  $ mu(M slash M') <= mu(M) - t. $
] <def:semisimple-basic-submodule>
We say $M'$ is basic in $M$ if $M'$ is 1-fold basic in $M$. An element $x in M$
is basic in $M$ if
$ mu(M slash A x) < mu(M), $
or equivalently,
$ mu(M slash A x) = mu(M) - 1. $
Thus, $x$ is a basic element of $M$ if and only if $A x$ is a basic submodule of
$M$. Basic elements are important because, under suitable hypotheses, a basic
element of a free module $F$ generates a free direct summand of $F$. See
Exercise @exc:basic-free-summand.

#lemma[
  Let $M$ be a nonzero module over a simple artinian ring $A$. Let
  $
    lambda(A) = lambda_0, quad "and" lambda(M) = p lambda_0 + q
    quad "where" 0 < q <= lambda_0.
  $
  Then
  #enum(
    numbering: "(i)",
    [$mu(M) = p + 1$,],
    [$M$ is a cyclic $A$-module if and only if $lambda(M) <= lambda_0$,],
    [An element $x in M$ is basic in $M$ if and only if $lambda(A x) >= q$.],
  )
] <lem:simple-generator-number-length>
#proof[
  Parts (i) and (ii) are trivial. For (iii), we have
  $
    x "basic in" M & <=> mu(M slash A x) <= mu(M) - 1 = p \
                   & <=> lambda(M slash A x) <= p lambda_0
                     <=> lambda(A x) >= lambda(M) - p lambda_0 = q.
  $
]

#lemma[
  Let $A$ be a simple artinian ring, and let $M = A m_1 plus.o A m_2$ be a
  cyclic $A$-module such that $A m_2$ is simple. Let $a in A$ be such that
  $ lambda(A(a, m_1)) >= lambda(M), $
  where $A(a, m_1)$ is the submodule of $A plus.o M$ generated by $(a, m_1)$.
  Then there exists an element $a' in A$ such that for each central unit
  $r in A$, we have
  $ M = A m_1 plus.o A a a' r m_2 = A(m_1 + a a' r m_2). $
] <lem:basic-simple-summand-adjustment>
#source(101)
#proof[
  Consider the exact sequence of left $A$-modules
  $ 0 -> (op("ann") m_1) a -> A(a, m_1) arrow.r^pi A m_1 -> 0, $
  where $pi$ is the projection map, and ann means $A$-annihilator. Then
  $ lambda(A m_1) = lambda(M) - 1 <= lambda(A(a, m_1)) - 1, $
  whence $(op("ann") m_1) a != 0$. But then $A = (op("ann") m_1) a A$, since the
  right-hand expression is a nonzero two-sided ideal of $A$. Therefore
  $ (op("ann") m_1) a A subset.not.eq op("ann") m_2, $
  so we may choose $b in op("ann") m_1$, $c in A$, such that
  $b a c in.not op("ann") m_2$. Therefore $b a c m_2 != 0$, and since $A m_2$ is
  simple, we obtain
  $ A m_2 = A b a c m_2 = A b a c r m_2 $
  for each central unit $r in A$. Furthermore, $b m_1 = 0$ gives
  $ A m_2 = A b(m_1 + a c r m_2) subset.eq A(m_1 + a c r m_2), $
  so $A(m_1 + a c r m_2)$ contains $m_2$, and hence also $m_1$. Therefore
  $M = A(m_1 + a c r m_2)$, as desired. Finally, the sum
  $ A m_1 + A a c r m_2 $
  is direct, since the second expression lies in $A m_2$. The sum contains
  $m_1 + a c r m_2$, hence coincides with $M$. Choosing $a' = c$, the proof is
  complete.
]

#lemma[
  Let $A$ be a simple artinian ring, $M != 0$ an $A$-module, and let $b in A$,
  $x, y in M$ be given. Then there exists an $a in A$ such that
  $ A(x + b a y) = A x plus.o A b a y, $ <eq:basic-adjustment-direct-sum>
  and
  $
    "either" y in A(x + b a y) quad "or" quad
    lambda(A(x + b a y)) >= lambda(A(b, x)),
  $
  <eq:basic-adjustment-length-alternative>
  where $(b, x) in A plus.o M$.
] <lem:basic-element-adjustment>
#proof[
  We put $d = lambda(A(b, x)) - lambda(A x)$. If $d <= 0$ we pick $a = 0$.
  Suppose now that $d > 0$, and use induction on $d$. If $y in A x$ we may again
  choose $a = 0$, so let us consider the case where $y in.not A x$, this is,
  $A x + A y supset A x$. We may write $A y$ as a sum of simple $A$-modules
  $A z$, and then $A x + A z supset A x$ for some $z$. Since $z in A y$, we may
  write $z = c y$ for some $c in A$, so we now have
  $ A x plus.o A c y supset A x, quad A c y = "simple" A"-module". $
  #source(102)
  Now $d > 0$ implies
  $
    lambda(A x plus.o A c y) = lambda(A x) + 1
    <= lambda(A(b, x)) <= lambda_0.
  $
  Therefore $A x plus.o A c y$ is a cyclic $A$-module by
  @lem:simple-generator-number-length, and we may thus apply
  @lem:basic-simple-summand-adjustment to the case where $M = A x plus.o A c y$,
  $m_1 = x$, $m_2 = c y$. Hence there exists an element $c' in A$ such that
  $ A x plus.o A c y = A(x + b c' c y) = A x plus.o A b c' c y. $
  Put $x' = x + b c' c y$, so
  $ A x plus.o A c y = A x' = A x plus.o A b c' c y. $
  Let
  $ d' = lambda(A(b, x')) - lambda(A x'); $
  then $d' = d - 1$, since $lambda(A x') = lambda(A x) + 1$, while
  $lambda(A(b, x)) = lambda(A(b, x'))$ by Exercise
  @exc:adjusted-pair-composition-length. By the induction hypothesis, there
  exists an $a' in A$ such that
  $ A(x' + b a' y) = A x' plus.o A b a' y, $
  and #named-equation(name: [$(ast)$])[$
    "either" y in A(x' + b a' y) quad "or" quad
    lambda(A(x' + b a' y)) >= lambda(A(b, x')) = lambda(A(b, x)).
  $] <eq:basic-adjustment-induction-alternative>
  We set
  $ x'' = x' + b a' y = x + b a y, quad "where" a = c' c + a', $
  and claim that this $a$ has the desired properties. First,
  $
    A x'' & = A(x' + b a' y) = A x' plus.o A b a' y
            = A x plus.o A b c' c y plus.o A b a' y \
          & = A x plus.o (A b c' c y plus.o A b a' y)
            supset.eq A x plus.o A b a y supset.eq A x'',
  $
  so @eq:basic-adjustment-direct-sum holds. Finally, by
  @eq:basic-adjustment-induction-alternative either $y in A x''$ or else
  $lambda(A x'') >= lambda(A(b, x))$. This establishes
  @eq:basic-adjustment-length-alternative and completes the proof.
]

We must now carry this information over to the semisimple case, and begin with
an easy preliminary result: #lemma[
  Let $A = product.co A_i$ be the Wedderburn decomposition of the semisimple
  artinian ring $A$, and let $M$ be any f.g. $A$-module. Write
  $M = product.co M_i$, where $M_i = A_i M$.
  #source(103)
  Then
  $
    mu_A lr((M)) = op("Max")_i mu_(A_i) lr((M_i)),
    quad mu_A lr((A plus.o M)) = mu_A lr((M)) + 1,
  $
  and $(1, m)$ is basic in $A plus.o M$ for each $m in M$.
] <lem:semisimple-generator-number>
#proof[
  The formula for $mu_A lr((M))$ is obvious, while that for
  $mu_A lr((A plus.o M))$ follows from @lem:simple-generator-number-length
  whenever $A$ is simple. For semisimple $A$, we have
  $
    mu(A plus.o M) = op("Max")_i mu(A_i plus.o M_i)
    = op("Max")_i mu(M_i) + 1 = mu(M) + 1.
  $
  Finally, for $m in M$ there is a surjection $M -> (A plus.o M) slash A(1, m)$,
  and therefore
  $ mu((A plus.o M) slash A(1, m)) <= mu(M) = mu(A plus.o M) - 1, $
  so $(1, m)$ is basic in $A plus.o M$.
]

#corollary[
  Keep the above notation, and let $N$ be a submodule of $M$. Put
  $ J = {i: mu_A lr((M)) = mu_(A_i) lr((M_i))}, $
  so $J$ is nonempty. Then
  #enum(
    numbering: "(a)",
    [$N$ is basic in $M$ if and only if $N_j$ is basic in $M_j$ for all
      $j in J$.],
    [Let $b in A$, $m in M$, and let $b_j, m_j$ denote images of $b, m$,
      respectively, under the projections $A -> A_j$, $M -> M_j$. Then $(b, m)$
      is basic in $A plus.o M$ if and only if $(b_j, m_j)$ is basic in
      $A_j plus.o M_j$ for all $j in J$.],
  )
] <cor:basic-semisimple-components>
#proof[
  Assume $N$ basic in $M$; then for all $j in J$,
  $ mu(M_j slash N_j) <= mu(M slash N) < mu(M) = mu(M_j), $
  so $N_j$ is basic in $M_j$. Conversely, if $N_j$ is basic in $M_j$ for all
  $j in J$, then for $i in.not J$ we have
  $ mu(M_i slash N_i) <= mu(M_i) < mu(M). $
  Hence
  $ mu(M slash N) = op("Max")_k {mu(M_k slash N_k)} < mu(M), $
  so $N$ is basic in $M$. Assertion (b) is a special case of (a).
]

#proposition[
  Let $M$ be a f.g. module over a semisimple ring $B$, and let
  $m_1, dots, m_n in M$, $b in B$, be such that
  $
    sum_1^n B m_i "is basic in" M, quad "and" quad
    (b, m_1) "is basic in" B plus.o M.
  $
  #source(104)
  Then there exist $a_2, dots, a_n in B$ such that
  $ m' = m_1 + b(a_2 m_2 + dots + a_n m_n) $
  is basic in $M$, and
  $ B m' = B m_1 plus.o product.co_2^n B b a_i m_i. $
] <prop:semisimple-basic-combination>
#proof[
  _Step 1._ (Reduction to the simple case). Let superscripts denote projections
  onto simple components, and put
  $ J = {j: mu_B lr((M)) = mu_(B^((j))) lr((M^((j))))}. $
  By the preceding results, the hypotheses carry over to the $B^((j))$-module
  $M^((j))$. Assuming that the proposition is valid for simple algebras, for
  each $j in J$ we can find elements $a_2^((j)), dots, a_n^((j)) in B^((j))$
  such that
  $ x^((j)) = m_1^((j)) + b^((j)) sum_(i=2)^n a_i^((j)) m_i^((j)) $
  is basic in $M^((j))$, and
  $
    B^((j)) x^((j)) = B^((j)) m_1^((j))
    plus.o product.co_(i=2)^n B^((j)) (b a_i m_i)^((j)).
  $
  We need only choose $a_i = sum_(j in J) a_i^((j))$, $2 <= i <= n$, and the
  desired result follows from @cor:basic-semisimple-components.

  _Step 2._ Assume now that $B$ is simple artinian, and use induction on $n$.
  There is nothing to prove when $n = 1$, so let $n > 1$ and assume the result
  holds at $n - 1$. By @lem:basic-element-adjustment, there exists an element
  $a_n in B$ such that
  $ B x = B m_1 plus.o B b a_n m_n, quad "where" x = m_1 + b a_n m_n, $
  and either $m_n in B x$ or else $lambda(B x) >= lambda(B(b, m_1))$. If
  $m_n in B x$, then $B x = B m_1 + B m_n$, so $B x + B m_2 + dots + B m_(n-1)$
  is basic in $M$. Further, since $(b, m_1)$ is basic in $B plus.o M$, so is
  $(b, x)$ by Exercise @exc:basic-pair-transvection. Applying the induction
  hypothesis, we may then choose $a_2, dots, a_(n-1) in B$ such that
  $ m' = x + b(a_2 m_2 + dots + a_(n-1) m_(n-1)) "is basic in" M, $
  and
  $
    B m' = B x plus.o product.co_2^(n-1) B b a_i m_i
    = B m_1 plus.o product.co_2^n B b a_i m_i,
  $
  so we are through in this case.
  #source(105)
  On the other hand, suppose that the alternative
  $lambda(B x) >= lambda(B(b, m_1))$ occurs. As in
  @lem:simple-generator-number-length, write $lambda(M) = p lambda_0 + q$, where
  $0 < q <= lambda_0$. Then
  $ lambda(B plus.o M) = (p + 1) lambda_0 + q. $
  But $(b, m_1)$ basic in $B plus.o M$ implies $lambda(B(b, m_1)) >= q$ by
  @lem:simple-generator-number-length. We thus have $lambda(B x) >= q$, so $x$
  is basic in $M$. Thus, in this case we just choose $a_2 = dots = a_(n-1) = 0$.
  This completes the proof.
]

#corollary[
  Let $M$ be a f.g. module over a semisimple ring $B$, and let
  $m_1, dots, m_n in M$, $b in B$, be such that
  $
    sum_1^n B m_i "is basic in" M, quad "and" quad
    (b, m_1) "is basic in" B plus.o M.
  $
  Then there exist $a_2, dots, a_n in B$ such that for all central units
  $r_2, dots, r_n in B$, the element
  $ m'' = m_1 + b(a_2 r_2 m_2 + dots + a_n r_n m_n) $
  is basic in $M$, and
  $ B m'' = B m_1 plus.o product.co_2^n B b a_i m_i. $
] <cor:basic-combination-central-units>
#proof[
  Let $m'$ be as in @prop:semisimple-basic-combination, let ${r_i}$ be a set of
  central units of $B$, and define $m''$ as above. Clearly $B m'' subset.eq
  B m'$, and we claim that equality holds. Consider the $B$-homomorphisms
  $
    B arrow.r^(m') B m_1 plus.o product.co_2^n B b a_i m_i
    arrow.r^((1, r_2, dots, r_n)) B m_1 plus.o product.co_2^n B b a_i m_i,
  $
  where the arrow labeled $m'$ is right multiplication by $m'$. Each map is
  surjective, whence so is the composite. This composite is right multiplication
  by $m''$, and thus $B m'' = B m'$ as claimed. Finally,
  $ mu(M slash B m'') = mu(M slash B m') < mu(M), $
  so $m''$ is basic in $M$.
]

The following basic proposition is due to Eisenbud–Evans @bib:Eisenbud1973.
#proposition[
  Let $M$ be a nonzero f.g. $B$-module, where $B$ is semisimple. Let
  $m_1, dots, m_n in M$, $b in B$, be such that
  $
    sum_1^n B m_i "is" t"-fold basic in" M, quad "and" quad
    (b, m_1) "is basic in" B plus.o M,
  $
  where $t < n$.
  #source(106)
  Then there exist elements $a_1, dots, a_(n-1) in B$ such that for all central
  units $r_i in B$, the module $N$ generated by
  $
    m_1 + b a_1 r_n m_n, quad m_2 + a_2 r_2 m_n,
    dots, m_(n-1) + a_(n-1) r_(n-1) m_n
  $
  is $t$-fold basic in $M$, and is independent of the choice of $r$’s.
] <prop:eisenbud-evans-semisimple-basic>
#proof[
  If $t = 0$, choose each $a_i = 0$. Now let $t = 1$, and use
  @cor:basic-combination-central-units; then there exist $c_2, dots, c_n in B$
  such that for all central units $r_2, dots, r_n in B$, the element
  $ x = m_1 + b(c_2 r_2 m_2 + dots + c_n r_n m_n) $
  is basic in $M$, and such that the module $B x$ does not depend on the choice
  of $r$’s. Hence $x, m_2, dots, m_(n-1)$ generate a basic submodule $N$ of $M$;
  but $N$ is also generated by
  $ m_1 + b c_n r_n m_n, quad m_2, dots, m_(n-1), $
  so we need only choose $a_1 = c_n$, $a_2 = 0, dots, a_(n-1) = 0$. This
  completes the proof when $t = 1$.

  Now let $t > 1$, and use induction. Construct $x$ as above, and set
  $overline(M) = M slash B x$. Then $mu(overline(M)) < mu(M)$ since $x$ is basic
  in $M$, and thus $mu(overline(M)) = mu(M) - 1$. If bars denote images in
  $overline(M)$, we have
  $
    mu(overline(M) slash sum_2^n B overline(m)_j)
    = mu(M slash sum_1^n B m_j) <= mu(M) - t = mu(overline(M)) - (t - 1),
  $
  so $overline(m)_2, dots, overline(m)_n$ generate a $(t - 1)$-fold basic
  submodule of $overline(M)$. By @lem:semisimple-generator-number,
  $(1, overline(m)_2)$ is basic in $B plus.o overline(M)$. The induction
  hypothesis thus guarantees the existence of elements $a_2, dots, a_(n-1) in B$
  such that
  $
    overline(m)_2 + a_2 r_2^(-1) r_n overline(m)_n, dots,
    overline(m)_(n-1) + a_(n-1) r_(n-1)^(-1) r_n overline(m)_n
  $
  generate a $(t - 1)$-fold basic submodule $overline(L)$ of $overline(M)$,
  which is independent of the $r$’s. Let $L$ be the submodule of $M$ defined as
  the inverse image of $overline(L)$ under the canonical mapping
  $M -> overline(M)$. Since
  $M slash L tilde.equiv overline(M) slash overline(L)$, it follows that $L$ is
  $t$-fold basic in $M$, and $L$ is independent of the choice of $r$’s. Now $L$
  is generated by
  $
    x, quad m_2 + a_2 r_2^(-1) r_n m_n, dots,
    m_(n-1) + a_(n-1) r_(n-1)^(-1) r_n m_n,
  $
  hence also by
  $
    m_1 + b a_1 r_n m_n, quad m_2 + a_2 r_2^(-1) r_n m_n, dots,
    m_(n-1) + a_(n-1) r_(n-1)^(-1) r_n m_n,
  $
  for suitably chosen $a_1$ (take $a_1 = c_n - sum_(i=2)^(n-1) c_i a_i$). These
  ${a_i}$ are the desired elements of $B$, and the proof is complete.
]

#source(107)
We are interested in basic elements for $R$-algebras over a commutative ring
$R$. For $P$ a prime ideal of $R$, let $R_P$ denote the localization of $R$ at
$P$.

#definition[
  Let $Lambda$ be an $R$-algebra, f.g./$R$ as module, and let $M$ be a f.g.
  $Lambda$-module. A submodule $M'$ of $M$ is called _$t$-fold basic at_ $P$ if
  $ mu(M_P slash M'_P) <= mu(M_P) - t, $
  where $mu(M_P)$ is the minimal number of generators of $M_P$ as
  $Lambda_P$-module. Basic means “1-fold basic.” If the condition holds for each
  prime ideal $P$ of $R$, we omit the words “at $P$”, and call $M'$ $t$-fold
  basic in $M$. An element $m in M$ is basic if
  $mu(M_P slash Lambda_P m) < mu(M_P)$ for each $P$.
] <def:basic-at-prime>

We shall use our results on modules over semisimple rings to prove:
#proposition[
  Let $Lambda$ be an $R$-algebra f.g./$R$, and let $P_1, dots, P_k$ be a set of
  distinct prime ideals of $R$. Let $M$ be a f.g. $Lambda$-module, and let
  $N = sum_1^n Lambda m_i subset.eq M$. Suppose that for some $a in Lambda$ we
  have for each $i$:
  $
    cases(
      N "is" t_i"-fold basic in" M "at" P_i,
      (a, m_1) "is basic in" Lambda plus.o M "at" P_i.
    )
  $
  Then there exist $a_1, dots, a_(n-1) in Lambda$ such that
  $ (a, m_1 + a a_1 m_n) "is basic in" Lambda plus.o M "at each" P_i, $
  <eq:basic-adjustment-at-primes>
  and such that the submodule of $M$ generated by
  $
    m_1 + a a_1 m_n, quad m_2 + a_2 m_n, dots,
    m_(n-1) + a_(n-1) m_n
  $
  is $min(t_i, n - 1)$-fold basic in $M$ at $P_i$, $1 <= i <= k$.
] <prop:basic-elements-finite-primes>
#proof[
  Exercise @exc:basic-pair-transvection yields @eq:basic-adjustment-at-primes
  for any choice of the ${a_j}$. Next, suppose that $t_i >= n$ for some $i$, so
  $sum_1^n Lambda m_j$ is $t_i$-fold basic (and hence $n$-fold basic) in $M$ at
  $P_i$. Then for any choice of $a$’s, the elements
  $
    m_n, quad m_1 + a a_1 m_n, quad m_2 + a_2 m_n, dots,
    m_(n-1) + a_(n-1) m_n
  $
  generate a submodule of $M$ which is $n$-fold basic in $M$ at $P_i$. It
  follows from Exercise @exc:generator-number-quotient-bound that the submodule
  generated by the last $n - 1$ of these elements is $(n - 1)$-fold basic in $M$
  at $P_i$, so again any choice of $a$’s will work for these ${P_i}$ where
  $t_i >= n$.

  It remains for us to consider those $P_i$ at which $t_i < n$, say
  ${P_1, dots, P_l}$, numbered so that $P_1$ is a minimal element of this set.
  Then $P_1 supset.not.eq (P_2 inter dots inter P_l)$, so we may choose $r in R$
  such that
  $ r in.not P_1, quad r in P_2 inter dots inter P_l. $
  #source(108)
  We use induction on $l$, and take $l > 0$ and assume the result holds at
  $l - 1$. Then there exist $a'_1, dots, a'_(n-1) in Lambda$ such that the
  elements
  $
    m'_1 = m_1 + a a'_1 m_n, quad m'_2 = m_2 + a'_2 m_n, dots,
    m'_(n-1) = m_(n-1) + a'_(n-1) m_n
  $
  generate a submodule of $M$ which is $t_i$-fold basic at $P_i$, $2 <= i <= l$.
  We intend to choose $b_1, dots, b_(n-1) in Lambda$ so that the elements
  $
    m'_1 + a b_1 r m_n, quad m'_2 + b_2 r m_n, dots,
    m'_(n-1) + b_(n-1) r m_n
  $ <eq:basic-prime-patching-generators>
  generate a submodule $M_0$ of $M$ which is $t_1$-fold basic at $P_1$. By
  Exercise @exc:local-generator-number-reductions, $M_0$ will also be $t_i$-fold
  basic at $P_i$, $2 <= i <= l$.

  We now let
  $
    overline(Lambda) = Lambda_(P_1) slash rad Lambda_(P_1), quad
    overline(M) = M_(P_1) slash (rad Lambda_(P_1)) M_(P_1).
  $
  By Exercise @exc:local-generator-number-reductions, it follows that
  $
    sum_1^n overline(Lambda) overline(m)_i "is" t_1"-fold basic in"
    overline(M), quad "and" quad
    (overline(a), overline(m)_1) "is basic in"
    overline(Lambda) plus.o overline(M).
  $
  As pointed out earlier, $(overline(a), overline(m)'_1)$ is also basic in
  $overline(Lambda) plus.o overline(M)$. Also,
  $
    sum_1^n overline(Lambda) overline(m)_i
    = sum_1^(n-1) overline(Lambda) overline(m)'_i
    + overline(Lambda) overline(r m)_n
    = t_1"-fold basic submodule of" overline(M),
  $
  where $t_1 < n$. By virtue of @prop:eisenbud-evans-semisimple-basic, there
  exist $a_1, dots, a_(n-1) in Lambda_(P_1)$ such that for each $s in R - P_1$,
  the elements
  $
    overline(m)'_1 + overline(a a_1 s r m)_n, quad
    overline(m)'_2 + overline(a_2 s r m)_n, dots,
    overline(m)'_(n-1) + overline(a_(n-1) s r m)_n
  $
  generate a $overline(Lambda)$-submodule of $overline(M)$ which is $t_1$-fold
  basic in $overline(M)$. Choose $s$ so that each $s a_i$ lies in the image of
  $Lambda$ in $Lambda_(P_1)$; then there exist elements ${b_i}$ in $Lambda$ so
  that the images of
  $
    m'_1 + a b_1 r m_n, quad m'_2 + b_2 r m_n, dots,
    m'_(n-1) + b_(n-1) r m_n
  $
  in $overline(M)$ generate a $t_1$-fold basic submodule. But then these
  elements generate a $t_1$-fold basic submodule of $M$ at $P_1$, as desired in
  @eq:basic-prime-patching-generators. This completes the proof of the
  proposition.
]

#source(109)
We are now going to specialize our discussion to the case where $R$ is a
Dedekind ring with quotient field $K$, in order to avoid some arguments from
commutative ring theory. All of the results carry over, with modifications in
the statements and proofs, to the case where $R$ is a commutative ring of finite
Krull dimension. (We say that $R$ has _Krull dimension_ $n$ if the largest
possible number of ideals in a strictly increasing chain of prime ideals of $R$
equals $n + 1$. Thus, if $R$ is a Dedekind domain and not a field, then $R$ has
Krull dimension 1.) We assume once and for all that $R != K$, to avoid
unnecessary complications in the statements of results. For $M$ any $R$-module,
denote $K times.o_R M$ by $K M$ for brevity (even though the map $M -> K M$ need
not be a monomorphism!).
#idx("Krull dimension")

#lemma[
  Let $R$ be a Dedekind domain with quotient field $K$, $Lambda$ an $R$-algebra
  f.g./$R$, and $N subset.eq M$ a pair of f.g. $Lambda$-modules. If $K N$ is
  $t$-fold basic in $K M$, then $N$ is $t$-fold basic in $M$ at almost all
  maximal ideals $P$ of $R$.
] <lem:basic-at-almost-all-primes>
#proof[
  For $n >= 0$, let
  $ I_n lr((M)) = sum_(M') op("ann")_R lr((M slash M')), $
  where $M'$ ranges over all submodules of $M$ which can be generated by $n$
  elements or fewer. Then $I_0 lr((M)) subset.eq I_1 lr((M)) subset.eq dots$,
  and
  $ I_k lr((M)) = R quad "whenever" mu(M) <= k. $
  Next, for any $P$, we have #named-equation(name: [$(ast)$])[$
    P supset.eq I_k lr((M slash N)) <=> mu(M_P slash N_P) > k.
  $] <eq:generator-ideal-local-criterion>
  Now let $k = mu(M slash N)$, and consider the chain #named-equation(
    name: [$(ast ast)$],
  )[$
    I_0 lr((M slash N)) subset.eq dots subset.eq I_k lr((M slash N)) = R.
  $] <eq:generator-ideal-chain>
  Let $P$ be a (nonzero) maximal ideal of $R$, and suppose that
  $mu(M_P slash N_P) = n$. If $n = 0$, then
  $ 0 <= mu(K M) - t <= mu(M_P) - t, $
  so $N$ is $t$-fold basic in $M$ at $P$. We may therefore assume $n > 0$. Then
  $
    P supset.eq I_(n-1) lr((M slash N)), quad
    P supset.not.eq I_n lr((M slash N)).
  $
  If $I_(n-1) = 0$, then @eq:generator-ideal-local-criterion shows that
  $mu(K M slash K N) >= n$, whence $mu(K M slash K N) = n$. But then
  $ mu(M_P slash N_P) = mu(K M slash K N) <= mu(K M) - t <= mu(M_P) - t, $
  so $N$ is $t$-fold basic in $M$ at $P$. On the other hand, if $I_(n-1) != 0$
  there are only finitely many $P$’s containing it. Thus, $N$ is $t$-fold basic
  in $M$ at each nonzero $P$, with the possible exception of those $P$’s which
  contain at least one nonzero ideal in the chain @eq:generator-ideal-chain.
  This proves the lemma.
]

We are now ready to prove one of the main results of Eisenbud–Evans:#idx(
  "Eisenbud-Evans Theorem",
)
#source(110)
#theorem[
  Let $R$ be a Dedekind domain with quotient field $K != R$, let $Lambda$ be an
  $R$-algebra f.g./$R$ as module, and let $M$ be any f.g. $Lambda$-module.
  Denote $K times.o_R M$ by $K M$, for brevity. Then:
  #enum(
    numbering: "(i)",
    [If $mu(K M) >= 2$, then $M$ contains a basic element.],
    [Let $N = sum_1^n Lambda m_i subset.eq M$, $a in Lambda$, and assume
      $
        cases(
          (a, m_1) "is basic in" Lambda plus.o M,
          N "is basic in" M "at each maximal ideal" P "of" R ", and",
          K N "is 2-fold basic in" K M.
        )
      $
      Then $M$ contains a basic element of the form $m_1 + a m'$, with
      $m' in sum_2^n Lambda m_i$.],
  )
] <th:eisenbud-evans-basic-element>
#idx("Eisenbud-Evans Theorem", target: [@th:eisenbud-evans-basic-element])
#proof[
  Since $K N$ is 2-fold basic in $K M$, surely $n >= 2$. By
  @lem:basic-at-almost-all-primes, $N$ is 2-fold basic in $M$ at all but
  finitely many maximal ideals ${P_1, dots, P_k}$ of $R$. By hypothesis, $N$ is
  basic in $M$ at each $P_i$, so by @prop:basic-elements-finite-primes there
  exist elements $a_1, dots, a_(n-1) in Lambda$ such that the $Lambda$-module
  $N'$ generated by the elements
  $
    m'_1 = m_1 + a a_1 m_n, quad m'_2 = m_2 + a_2 m_n, dots,
    m'_(n-1) = m_(n-1) + a_(n-1) m_n,
  $
  is basic in $M$ at each $P_i$, and is $min(n - 1, 2)$-fold basic in $M$ at the
  ideal $P_0 = {0}$. Further, $(a, m'_1)$ is basic in $Lambda plus.o M$ by
  Exercise @exc:basic-pair-transvection. We claim that $N'$ is basic in $M$ at
  each maximal ideal $P$, and it suffices to check this when
  $P in.not {P_1, dots, P_k}$. For such $P$, $N' + Lambda m_n$ is 2-fold basic
  in $M$ at $P$, whence surely $N'$ is 1-fold basic in $M$ at $P$, as desired.

  Thus, if $n > 2$ we have a new submodule $N'$ of $M$, generated by $n - 1$
  elements, and satisfying the same conditions as does $N$. Hence we may iterate
  the procedure until we reach the case $n = 2$, at which point $n - 1 = 1$, and
  the element $m'_1$ above is basic in $M$. This completes the proof of (ii).
  But then (i) is clear, since we need only choose $N = M$ and $a = 1$ (see
  Exercise @exc:unit-pair-basic-at-primes).
]

#remarks(numbered: false)[
  #enum(
    numbering: "(1)",
    [Let $R$ be a commutative noetherian ring of finite Krull dimension $d$. For
      each prime ideal $P$ of $R$, let $dim P$ be the largest integer $m$ for
      which there exists a chain of prime ideals of $R$
      $ P subset P_1 subset dots subset P_m. $
      Let $Lambda$ be an $R$-algebra, f.g./$R$ as module, and let $M$ be an f.g.
      $Lambda$-module. Then the generalization of the preceding theorem is as
      follows:
      #enum(
        numbering: "(i)",
        [If $mu(M_P) > d$ for each minimal $P$, then $M$ contains a basic
          element.],
        [Let $N = sum_1^n Lambda m_i subset.eq M$, $a in Lambda$, and assume
          $
            cases(
              (a, m_1) "is basic in" Lambda plus.o M ", and",
              N "is" (dim P + 1)"-fold basic in" M
              "for each prime ideal" P "of" R.
            )
          $
          Then $M$ contains a basic element of the form $m_1 + a m'$, with
          $m' in sum_2^n Lambda m_i$.],
      )],
    [The hypothesis that $R$ be noetherian of finite Krull dimension can be
      replaced by the weaker hypothesis that $op("m-spec") R$ is noetherian and
      of dimension $d$.],
    [The hypothesis that $(a, m_1)$ be basic in $Lambda plus.o M$ is always
      fulfilled when $a = 1$.],
  )
] <rem:basic-element-finite-dimension>
#idx("Dimension of prime ideal", target: [@rem:basic-element-finite-dimension])
#source(111)

We are now ready to show that the Eisenbud–Evans Theorem gives the four main
results stated at the start of this section. In fact, we obtain these results
not only for $R$-orders, but more generally for $R$-algebras, f.g./$R$ as
module. We begin with:

#theorem(title: [Serre])[
  Let $Lambda$ be an $R$-algebra as in @th:eisenbud-evans-basic-element, and let
  $M$ be a f.g. projective $Lambda$-module. Suppose that $K Lambda^((2)) | K M$,
  and that $Lambda_P | M_P$ for each maximal ideal $P$ of $R$. Then
  $Lambda | M$. Further, if $M = sum_1^n Lambda m_i$, the generator of the free
  summand of $M$ may be chosen in the form $m_1 + a_2 m_2 + dots + a_n m_n$,
  $a_i in Lambda$.
] <th:serre-free-summand>
#idx("Serre's Theorem", target: [@th:serre-free-summand])
#proof[
  The assertion is trivial if $Lambda = 0$, so assume $Lambda != 0$. The second
  assertion implies the first. Choose a f.g. projective $Lambda$-module $L$ such
  that $F = M plus.o L$ is $Lambda$-free.

  Suppose first that $K Lambda = 0$. Then some nonzero element of $R$
  annihilates $Lambda$, so $Lambda$ is artinian and
  $cal(S) = {P: Lambda_P != 0}$ is finite.#ed-note[
    For $R = ZZ$, $Lambda = FF_2$ and $P = 3 ZZ$, we have $Lambda_P = 0$, and
    the strict inequality defining basicness becomes $0 < 0$. Such primes must
    be omitted when testing free summands. Likewise, generic 2-fold basicness
    cannot be used when $K Lambda = 0$.
  ] At every $P in cal(S)$, the hypothesis $Lambda_P | M_P$ shows that $M_P$ is
  basic in $F_P$, and $(1, m_1)$ is basic in $Lambda_P plus.o F_P$. Apply
  @prop:basic-elements-finite-primes successively to the generators of $M$, with
  $t_P = 1$ for each $P in cal(S)$. We obtain an element
  $m = m_1 + a_2 m_2 + dots + a_n m_n$ which is basic in $F$ at each such $P$.
  By the local argument of Exercise @exc:basic-free-summand, $Lambda_P m$ is a
  free direct summand of $F_P$ with basis $m$. Outside $cal(S)$ both modules
  vanish. Thus $Lambda m tilde.equiv Lambda$ and $Lambda m | F$; restricting a
  retraction $F -> Lambda m$ to $M$ gives $Lambda m | M$, as desired.

  We may now assume $K Lambda != 0$. At each maximal ideal $P$ we have
  $M_P = Lambda_P plus.o X$ for some $X$, whence
  $ mu(M_P plus.o L_P) >= 1 + mu(L_P), $
  and so $M$ is basic in $M plus.o L$ at each $P$. Likewise, $K M$ is 2-fold
  basic in $K(M plus.o L)$. Further, $(1, m_1)$ is basic in
  $Lambda plus.o (M plus.o L)$. It follows from Theorem
  @th:eisenbud-evans-basic-element that $M$ contains a basic element $m$ of
  $M plus.o L$ of the desired form. Then $Lambda m | (M plus.o L)$ by Exercise
  @exc:basic-free-summand, whence $Lambda m | M$, which proves the corollary.
]

In the same vein, we prove: #theorem(name: "Bass Cancellation Theorem")[
  Let $M, Lambda$ be as above, and let $L, N$ be f.g. projective
  $Lambda$-modules such that $M plus.o N tilde.equiv L plus.o N$. Then
  $M tilde.equiv L$.
] <th:bass-cancellation>
#proof[
  If $K Lambda = 0$, then $Lambda$ is artinian, and the Krull–Schmidt–Azumaya
  Theorem cancels $N$ directly. We may therefore assume $K Lambda != 0$.

  Increasing $N$ by a suitable summand, we may assume that $N$ is $Lambda$-free.
  It then suffices to prove the result when $N = Lambda$, so let
  $
    alpha: Lambda plus.o L tilde.equiv Lambda plus.o M,
    quad alpha(1, 0) = (a, m_1),
  $
  be given. We shall set $beta = psi alpha$ for some suitable
  $psi in Aut(Lambda plus.o M)$, chosen so that $beta|_Lambda = 1$. Then
  $beta: Lambda plus.o L tilde.equiv Lambda plus.o M$ induces an isomorphism
  $L tilde.equiv M$, and we are through.

  Since $(1, 0)$ is basic in $Lambda plus.o L$, the element $(a, m_1)$ is basic
  in $Lambda plus.o M$. Write $M = sum_1^n Lambda m_i$; the preceding results
  imply that $M$ has a free direct summand $Lambda m$, where $m = m_1 + a m'$
  for some $m' in sum_2^n Lambda m_i$. Now define $psi in Aut(Lambda plus.o M)$
  by composition of maps:
  #cancellation-transvections
  #source(112)
  where the $Lambda$-maps $f, g, h$ are defined thus:
  $ f(x) = x m', quad h(x) = -x m, quad x in Lambda, $
  and $g in Hom(M, Lambda)$ is given by composition
  $ M = Lambda m plus.o M_0 -> Lambda m -> Lambda -> Lambda(1 - a). $
  Then we have $g(m) = 1 - a$, so
  $
    psi(a, m_1) & = mat(1, 0; h, 1) mat(1, g; 0, 1) mat(1, 0; f, 1)(a, m_1) \
                & = mat(1, g; h, h g + 1)(a, f a + m_1)
                  = mat(1, g; h, h g + 1)(a, m) \
                & = (a + g m, h a + (h g + 1)m) = (1, 0).
  $
  Then
  $ beta(1, 0) = psi alpha(1, 0) = psi(a, m_1) = (1, 0). $
  so $beta|_Lambda = 1$, and the proof is finished.
]

#remark(numbered: false)[
  The generalized versions of the preceding two theorems, for the case of a
  commutative noetherian ring $R$ of finite Krull dimension $d$, are as follows:
  In @th:serre-free-summand, the hypotheses should read:
  $ Lambda_P^((1 + dim P)) | M_P quad "for each" P. $
  The conclusion is unchanged. The same change in hypothesis occurs in
  @th:bass-cancellation.
] <rem:serre-cancellation-finite-dimension>

Our next consequence of Theorem @th:eisenbud-evans-basic-element is: #theorem(
  title: [Swan–Forster],
)[
  Let $Lambda$ be an $R$-algebra as in @th:eisenbud-evans-basic-element, let $P$
  range over all maximal ideals of $R$, and put
  $ t = op("Max")_P {mu(M_P), 1 + mu(K M)} $
  (omit the term $1 + mu(K M)$ if $K M = 0$). Then $mu(M) <= t$.
] <th:swan-forster-dedekind>
#idx("Swan-Forster Theorem", target: [@th:swan-forster-dedekind])
#proof[
  If $Lambda = 0$, the assertion is trivial. If $K Lambda = 0$, then $Lambda$ is
  artinian, as in the proof of @th:serre-free-summand, and
  $cal(S) = {P: Lambda_P != 0}$ is finite. Decomposition over these supported
  primes gives
  $
    Lambda tilde.equiv product_(P in cal(S)) Lambda_P,
    quad M tilde.equiv product_(P in cal(S)) M_P.
  $
  Padding generator lists by zeros in the shorter components shows that
  $ mu(M) = op("Max")_(P in cal(S)) mu(M_P). $
  Since $K M = 0$, this proves the asserted bound in this case. We may therefore
  assume $K Lambda != 0$.

  (In the special case where $M$ is a nonzero ideal of $R$, we have
  $mu(M_P) = 1$ for each $P$, and $1 + mu(K M) = 2$, so $t = 2$. We recover the
  classical result that $M$ can be generated by 2 elements.)

  Let $n = mu(M)$; we assume that $n > t$, and try to obtain a contradiction.
  There is an exact sequence
  $ 0 -> N -> F -> M -> 0, quad "where" F tilde.equiv Lambda^((n)). $
  #source(113)
  At each maximal ideal $P$ of $R$, we have
  $ mu(F_P slash N_P) = mu(M_P) = mu(F_P) - (n - mu(M_P)) <= mu(F_P) - 1, $
  so $N$ is basic in $F$ at $P$. A similar argument shows that $K N$ is 2-fold
  basic in $K F$. By Theorem @th:eisenbud-evans-basic-element it follows that
  $N$ contains an element $x$ which is basic in $F$. Then
  $Lambda x tilde.equiv Lambda$, and $F = Lambda x plus.o F'$ by Exercise
  @exc:basic-free-summand. Thus we have
  $ F = Lambda^((n-1)) plus.o Lambda tilde.equiv F' plus.o Lambda x. $
  If $n >= 3$, the Bass Cancellation Theorem gives
  $F' tilde.equiv Lambda^((n-1))$. But then
  $
    F' tilde.equiv F slash Lambda x arrow.r^theta F slash N tilde.equiv M,
    quad "with" theta "surjective",
  $
  so $F'$ maps onto $M$, whence $mu(M) <= n - 1$, a contradiction.

  On the other hand, if $n = 2$ then (assuming $M != 0$) we have $t = 1$ and
  necessarily $K M = 0$. If $frak(a) = op("ann")_R M$, then $frak(a)$ is
  nonzero; let
  $
    overline(R) = R slash frak(a), quad
    overline(Lambda) = Lambda slash frak(a) Lambda,
  $
  so $overline(Lambda)$ is an $overline(R)$-algebra, and $M$ is a
  $overline(Lambda)$-module. The preceding argument shows that $F'$ maps onto
  $M$, whence also $overline(F)'$ maps onto $M$. But
  $overline(F) tilde.equiv overline(F)' plus.o overline(Lambda)$, and the
  Krull–Schmidt–Azumaya Theorem holds for f.g. $overline(Lambda)$-modules,
  whence $overline(F)' tilde.equiv overline(Lambda)^((n-1))$. This shows again
  that $mu(M) <= n - 1$, a contradiction, and the proof is complete.
]

Finally, we prove Bass’s result: #theorem(name: "Bass Stable Range Theorem")[
  Let $Lambda$ be an $R$-algebra as in @th:eisenbud-evans-basic-element, and
  suppose that
  $ Lambda = Lambda b_1 + dots + Lambda b_n, quad "where" n > 2. $
  Then there exist $x_1, dots, x_(n-1) in Lambda$ such that
  $
    Lambda = Lambda(b_1 + x_1 b_n) + Lambda(b_2 + x_2 b_n)
    + dots + Lambda(b_(n-1) + x_(n-1) b_n).
  $
] <th:bass-stable-range-dedekind>
#idx("Stable range", target: [@th:bass-stable-range-dedekind])
#idx("Stability theorems", target: [@th:bass-stable-range-dedekind])
#proof[
  The assertion is trivial if $Lambda = 0$, so assume $Lambda != 0$. We use the
  basic-element results for right $Lambda$-modules, that is, for left modules
  over the $R$-algebra $Lambda^"op"$. Let $bold(b) = (b_1, dots, b_n)$, so
  $bold(b)$ is unimodular by hypothesis. By @lem:unimodular-free-direct-summand,
  this is equivalent to the assertion that $bold(b) Lambda$ is a free direct
  summand of the right $Lambda$-module $Lambda^((n))$, or equivalently (see
  Exercises @exc:basic-free-summand, @exc:free-summand-basic), that $bold(b)$ is
  basic in $Lambda^((n))$ at each maximal ideal $P$ of $R$ for which
  $Lambda_P != 0$.

  We now set $M = Lambda^((n-1))$. Since $n - 1 >= 2$, it follows that $M$ is
  2-fold basic in $Lambda^((n))$ at every such maximal ideal $P$, and also at
  the zero ideal if $K Lambda != 0$. We put
  $
    m_1 = (b_1, dots, b_(n-1)), quad m_2 = (1, 0, dots, 0), dots,
    m_n = (0, dots, 0, 1),
  $
  viewed as elements of $M$. Then $(b_n, m_1) in Lambda plus.o M$, and
  $(b_n, m_1)$ is unimodular, hence basic in $Lambda plus.o M$ at every maximal
  ideal $P$ for which $Lambda_P != 0$.
  #source(114)
  If $K Lambda = 0$, then $Lambda$ is artinian and $cal(S) = {P: Lambda_P != 0}$
  is finite. Apply @prop:basic-elements-finite-primes successively at these
  primes to the generators $m_1, dots, m_n$, with $t_P = 1$. If $K Lambda != 0$,
  apply Theorem @th:eisenbud-evans-basic-element as before. In either case there
  exist $x_1, dots, x_(n-1) in Lambda$ such that
  $ m' = m_1 + (m_2 x_1 + dots + m_n x_(n-1)) b_n $
  is basic in $M$ at every maximal ideal $P$ with $Lambda_P != 0$. By the local
  argument of Exercise @exc:basic-free-summand, this $m'$ is unimodular at those
  primes. Outside this finite support in the artinian case the modules vanish,
  so $m'$ is unimodular over $Lambda$ as well. But
  $ m' = (b_1 + x_1 b_n, b_2 + x_2 b_n, dots, b_(n-1) + x_(n-1) b_n), $
  so we have obtained our desired unimodular $(n - 1)$-tuple. This completes the
  proof.
]

#corollary(title: [Surjective Stability])[
  Let $Lambda$ be an $R$-algebra, f.g./$R$ as module, where $R$ is a Dedekind
  domain. Then $Lambda$ has stable range 2, and the map
  $ GL_2 lr((Lambda)) -> K_1 lr((Lambda)) $
  is surjective.
] <cor:surjective-stability-dedekind>
#idx("Surjective stability", target: [@cor:surjective-stability-dedekind])

#remarks(numbered: false)[
  For the case where $R$ is any commutative noetherian ring of Krull dimension
  $d$, the generalized versions of the preceding results are as follows:
] <rem:stable-range-finite-dimension>

#theorem(title: [Swan–Forster])[
  Let $R$ have Krull dimension $d$, and let $Lambda$ be an $R$-algebra, f.g./$R$
  as module. Let $M$ be any f.g. $Lambda$-module, and set
  $ t = op("Max")_P {mu(M_P) + dim P}, $
  where $P$ ranges over all prime ideals of $R$ such that $M_P != 0$. Then
  $ mu(M) <= t. $
] <th:swan-forster-finite-dimension>

#theorem(name: "Bass Stable Range Theorem")[
  Let $Lambda$ be as above, and suppose that
  $ Lambda = Lambda b_1 + dots + Lambda b_n, quad "where" n > d + 1. $
  Then there exist $x_1, dots, x_(n-1) in Lambda$ such that
  $
    Lambda = Lambda(b_1 + x_1 b_n) + Lambda(b_2 + x_2 b_n)
    + dots + Lambda(b_(n-1) + x_(n-1) b_n).
  $
  In other words, $Lambda$ has stable range $d + 1$, and the map
  $ GL_n lr((Lambda)) -> K_1 lr((Lambda)) $
  is surjective for $n >= d + 1$.
] <th:bass-stable-range-finite-dimension>

#remarks(numbered: false, name: "Notes")[
  #enum(
    numbering: "(1)",
    [Let $Lambda = ZZ G$, where $|G| = n$. Let $M$ and $N$ be $Lambda$-lattices
      such that
      $ M plus.o Lambda^((k)) tilde.equiv N plus.o Lambda^((k)) $
      for some $k$.
      #source(115)
      Suppose that neither $M$ nor $N$ is a cyclic $Lambda$-module. Then (see
      Cohen @bib:Cohen1978) $mu_Lambda lr((M)) = mu_Lambda lr((N))$. Further,
      when $n > 1$, $M$ contains a sublattice $L$ such that
      $ mu_Lambda lr((L)) = op("Max")_(p divides n) {mu_(Lambda_p) lr((M_p))}. $
      (Compare with the Swan–Forster Theorem @th:swan-forster-dedekind.)],
    [Let $M$ be a $Lambda$-lattice, where $Lambda = ZZ G$, and let $|G| = n$.
      Set
      $
        t = op("Max")_(p divides n) {mu_(Lambda_p) lr((M_p))}
        quad (n > 1),
      $
      and assume that $t >= 2$. Suppose that every simple $QQ G$-module (except
      possibly the trivial module $QQ$) occurs more often in $(QQ G)^((t))$ than
      in $QQ M$. Then $mu_Lambda lr((M)) = t$. The result is due to Swan
      @bib:Swan1965, Lemma 4.4; see also Gruenberg @bib:Gruenberg1976, Theorem
      7.3.],
  )
] <rem:integral-group-ring-generator-bounds>

#exercises-heading
#exercise[
  Let $M$ be a f.g. $A$-module, where $A$ is any ring, and let
  $(a, m) in A plus.o M$ be such that
  $ mu((A plus.o M) slash A(a, m)) < mu(A plus.o M). $
  Show that this same inequality holds when $m$ is replaced by $m + a m'$, for
  any $m' in M$.
  #hint([@exc:basic-pair-transvection])[
    The map
    $ (a, x) -> (a, x + a m'), quad a in A, quad x in M, $
    is an $A$-automorphism of $A plus.o M$ carrying $(a, m)$ onto
    $(a, m + a m')$.
  ]
] <exc:basic-pair-transvection>

#exercise[
  Let $M$ be a f.g. $A$-module, where $A$ is an artinian ring, and let $a in A$,
  $m, m' in M$. Show that the submodules $A(a, m)$ and $A(a, m + a m')$ of
  $A plus.o M$ have the same composition length.
  #hint([@exc:adjusted-pair-composition-length])[
    There is an $A$-exact sequence
    $ 0 -> op("ann")(a, m) -> A -> A(a, m) -> 0, $
    and another in which $m$ is replaced by $m + a m'$. But $(a, m)$ and
    $(a, m + a m')$ have the same annihilator.
  ]
] <exc:adjusted-pair-composition-length>

#exercise[
  Let $Lambda$ be an $R$-algebra f.g./$R$, where $R$ is any commutative ring.
  Let $F tilde.equiv Lambda^((n))$ be $Lambda$-free of rank $n$, and let
  $x in F$ be basic in $F$ at each maximal ideal $P$ of $R$ (that is,
  $mu(F_P slash Lambda_P x) < mu(F_P)$ for each $P$). Show that $Lambda x$ is a
  free direct summand of $F$.
  #hint([@exc:basic-free-summand])[
    We must show that $F slash Lambda x$ is projective, and that the surjection
    $alpha: Lambda -> Lambda x$ is an isomorphism.
    #source(116)
    It suffices to establish these facts at each $P$, so after localizing at
    $P$, we may assume $R$ is local and $mu(F slash Lambda x) = mu(F) - 1$.
    Hence there is a surjection $gamma: Lambda^((n-1)) -> F slash Lambda x$.
    Using the Horseshoe Lemma, we obtain a commutative diagram with exact rows:
    #basic-free-summand-diagram
    Now use the Snake Lemma to show that $alpha, beta$ and $gamma$ are
    isomorphisms.
  ]
] <exc:basic-free-summand>

#exercise[
  Keep the notation of Exercise @exc:basic-free-summand. Show that every free
  direct summand of positive rank of $Lambda^((n))$ is basic in $Lambda^((n))$
  at each $P$ for which $Lambda_P != 0$.
] <exc:free-summand-basic>

#exercise[
  Let $M$ be a f.g. $A$-module, where $A$ is any ring, and let
  $m_1, dots, m_n in M$. Show that
  $ mu(M slash sum_1^n A m_i) >= mu(M) - n. $
  Further, if equality holds, then also
  $ mu(M slash sum_1^r A m_i) = mu(M) - r $
  for $1 <= r <= n$.
] <exc:generator-number-quotient-bound>

#exercise[
  Let $M$ be a f.g. $A$-module, where $A$ is any ring, and let
  $overline(A) = A slash rad A$, $overline(M) = M slash (rad A)M$. Show that
  $mu_A lr((M)) = mu_(overline(A)) lr((overline(M)))$, where $mu$ denotes
  minimal number of generators.
] <exc:generator-number-radical-quotient>

#exercise[
  Let $R$ be a commutative local ring with maximal ideal $P$, and $Lambda$ an
  $R$-algebra f.g./$R$ as module. Let $M$ be an f.g. $Lambda$-module, and let
  bars denote reduction modulo $rad Lambda$. Let
  $tilde(Lambda) = Lambda slash P Lambda$, $tilde(M) = M slash P M$. Show that
  $
    mu_Lambda lr((M)) = mu_(tilde(Lambda)) lr((tilde(M)))
    = mu_(overline(Lambda)) lr((overline(M))).
  $
  If $N = sum_1^k Lambda m_i$ is a submodule of $M$, and
  $N' = sum_1^k Lambda m'_i$ another submodule such that $m'_i == m_i (mod P M)$
  for each $i$, show that
  $ mu(M slash N) = mu(M slash N'). $
] <exc:local-generator-number-reductions>

#exercise[
  Let $Lambda$ be an $R$-algebra f.g./$R$, where $R$ is commutative, and let $M$
  be any f.g. $Lambda$-module. Show that for any $m in M$, the element $(1, m)$
  is basic in $Lambda plus.o M$ at every prime ideal $P$ of $R$ for which
  $Lambda_P != 0$.
] <exc:unit-pair-basic-at-primes>

#exercise[
  Give an example of a f.g. $A$-module $M$ (for some suitably chosen ring $A$)
  such that $mu(A plus.o M) != mu(M) + 1$.
] <exc:generator-number-nonadditivity>
